        "message" : [
          "%md",
          "",
          "## Findings",
          "",
          "### Finding 1: The Labels Stop at the First Hop",
          "",
          "The red team labels contain no chains: every labeled compromise starts at a staging host (Step 1). The victims still move on. 148 victims of `C17693` connect onward within an hour after their compromise and reach 109 computers with another account (Step 4). This onward movement carries no label.",
          "",
          "### Finding 2: Time Ordering Turns Reach into a Reviewable List",
          "",
          "Without time ordering, `C17693` reaches 7,754 computers within two hops (Scenario 2). With time ordering, its victims reach 151 computers in the hour after their compromise. An analyst can review that list.",
          "",
          "### Finding 3: Two Credential Origins",
          "",
          "Most accounts of the onward hops had logged on to the victim days before the compromise, so their credentials were there to collect (Step 3). On `C1567`, 17 accounts appear only after the compromise and are used within the hour: the attacker brought them along.",
          "",
          "### Finding 4: Relay Hosts Find Confirmed Victims Without Labels",
          "",
          "The chain search in Step 5 uses no labels, yet 33 of its 132 relay hosts are confirmed victims, including 4 of the top 5 (Step 5c). The ranking by account switches favors servers that many accounts log on to. `C4313` is the strongest unlabeled candidate.",
          "",
          "### Finding 5: The Chains End at the Core",
          "",
          "The computers with the highest PageRank at the end of the chains are the central computers of the network, and four of them follow a chain with a confirmed incoming hop (Step 6b)."
        ],
